{"kind":"request","text":"Lead the documentation with the solo-developer-in-an-organization route, alongside the shared-genesis one. Hugh's judgement, 2026-08-10: the easiest onboarding is a developer running gs init on a local copy of a repository they already work in, with their own agents, and no expectation that the organization stands up a central sequencer. The full everyone-on-the-same-genesis model is functionally dependent on containerization, which is not planned yet, so today it is the aspiration and the solo route is the supported path. The documentation does not say this. Every entry path starts from an empty directory: docs/how-to/end-to-end.md opens with mktemp -d, and the docs map sends a new reader there second after why.md. The work loop pages presume several actors and ratification. Nothing covers adopting gitseq alone, inside an organization that has adopted nothing, which is the case almost every first reader is actually in, and the omission reads as a precondition: it implies you need your organization to agree before you can begin. Put the solo route first and the shared-genesis route beside it, and say which one the current build supports.","body":{"conditions":"Sequenced after the ownership-descent identity work lands, because that change rewrites the first-use provisioning story any onboarding page would describe. Then: README.md and docs/README.md present two adoption routes side by side, solo-in-an-organization first, shared-genesis second, with the honest reason for the order stated rather than implied. A how-to page covers the solo route from a repository the reader already works in, not from an empty directory, and its commands are runnable so the docset example gate executes them and thereby proves the claim that gs init works in an existing repository with history. The shared-genesis route says plainly that it presumes infrastructure the project does not yet plan, so a reader can tell which route is supported today. docs/getting-started.md keeps the public clone line the root public-surface test pins, every touched page keeps front matter with live rests_on anchors, and the docset surface, example, anchor and flare gates all pass. Full gates and a clean merge-tree against current main; ready-for-review report offers the exact head.","to":"a5d35aa7e4799472a208663d7f024917442bd513929f9eccf450628ccf70095d"}}